WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.480 --> 00:00:09.680
- Thank you all for coming.

00:00:09.680 --> 00:00:13.720
Welcome to this new
series that we've launched

00:00:13.720 --> 00:00:16.640
with the engineering workforce development

00:00:16.640 --> 00:00:18.320
of the Center for Quantum Network

00:00:18.320 --> 00:00:20.350
and the Yale Quantum Institute.

00:00:20.350 --> 00:00:21.380
We are delighted to be here.

00:00:21.380 --> 00:00:22.790
My name is Florian Carle.

00:00:22.790 --> 00:00:27.010
I'm the manager of the
Yale Quantum Institute.

00:00:27.010 --> 00:00:29.250
And I will be moderating this panel

00:00:30.400 --> 00:00:32.740
with my colleague, Russell Balliet

00:00:32.740 --> 00:00:35.900
So this series, we are
kick-starting the series

00:00:35.900 --> 00:00:39.520
with one event today
about prepare for college

00:00:39.520 --> 00:00:43.250
because we've realized that
building quantum technology

00:00:43.250 --> 00:00:46.190
is something that requires a lot of people

00:00:46.190 --> 00:00:47.180
with different backgrounds.

00:00:47.180 --> 00:00:49.750
We need physicists, we
need electrical engineer,

00:00:49.750 --> 00:00:53.290
we need computer scientists,
software engineer, chemist.

00:00:53.290 --> 00:00:55.050
There's a lot of traits that we need

00:00:55.050 --> 00:00:57.860
and it can be very overwhelming
to consider a career

00:00:57.860 --> 00:00:59.950
in quantum science and information.

00:00:59.950 --> 00:01:02.480
And so by attending this session today,

00:01:02.480 --> 00:01:04.150
you will hear from current students,

00:01:04.150 --> 00:01:05.640
from staff and faculty in the field,

00:01:05.640 --> 00:01:08.170
what it's like to work in quantum.

00:01:08.170 --> 00:01:10.140
And hopefully you can get advice

00:01:10.140 --> 00:01:13.930
on navigating the various
pathway that you can take.

00:01:13.930 --> 00:01:18.120
So this event will be recorded.

00:01:18.120 --> 00:01:20.953
So a little bit of technical
details on the zoom.

00:01:22.504 --> 00:01:25.320
You can only see and hear the panelists.

00:01:25.320 --> 00:01:28.400
You have a feature at
the bottom of your screen

00:01:28.400 --> 00:01:32.160
that has the Q and A panel.

00:01:32.160 --> 00:01:33.272
So if you click on it,

00:01:33.272 --> 00:01:35.630
you should be able to type your questions.

00:01:35.630 --> 00:01:37.940
And then you can also put a thumbs up

00:01:37.940 --> 00:01:40.010
on the question you want answer the most.

00:01:40.010 --> 00:01:42.780
And so the most requested
question will go to the top

00:01:42.780 --> 00:01:45.480
and then Russell and
I will go through them

00:01:45.480 --> 00:01:46.813
and ask to the panelists.

00:01:49.827 --> 00:01:52.400
There's also closed
captioning if you want,

00:01:52.400 --> 00:01:54.480
and this will be recorded

00:01:54.480 --> 00:01:57.023
and you can watch it later on demand.

00:01:58.180 --> 00:01:59.300
Before we start,

00:01:59.300 --> 00:02:02.590
I would like to introduce
my colleague Russell Balliet

00:02:02.590 --> 00:02:05.100
and he will be the
second moderator with me.

00:02:05.100 --> 00:02:06.650
And he's gonna say a few words.

00:02:07.510 --> 00:02:08.480
- Thanks, Florian.

00:02:08.480 --> 00:02:09.940
So yeah, I'm Russell Balliet.

00:02:09.940 --> 00:02:14.100
I'm with the center for quantum
networks and specifically

00:02:14.100 --> 00:02:16.650
with the engineering
workforce development team.

00:02:16.650 --> 00:02:18.810
I'm the assistant director.

00:02:18.810 --> 00:02:20.110
So just real quick a little bit

00:02:20.110 --> 00:02:22.110
about the center for quantum networks.

00:02:22.110 --> 00:02:26.670
It's a national science
foundation funded center

00:02:26.670 --> 00:02:30.220
that just got started late last year.

00:02:30.220 --> 00:02:35.130
And we are a part of 10
different institutions

00:02:35.130 --> 00:02:37.520
or, sorry there are 10
institutions in our center

00:02:37.520 --> 00:02:38.920
including Yale.

00:02:38.920 --> 00:02:41.490
I'm specifically with the
University of Arizona,

00:02:41.490 --> 00:02:43.700
which is the lead institution.

00:02:43.700 --> 00:02:47.360
And so the center for
quantum networks got started

00:02:47.360 --> 00:02:51.410
because one of our big goals
is to help lay the foundation

00:02:51.410 --> 00:02:54.060
for a socially responsible
quantum internet,

00:02:54.060 --> 00:02:56.620
which some of you have
probably heard about

00:02:56.620 --> 00:03:00.660
hoping that this is gonna spur
new technology industries,

00:03:00.660 --> 00:03:03.800
competitive barter plays for
quantum service providers

00:03:03.800 --> 00:03:08.590
and application developers
for the benefit of all.

00:03:08.590 --> 00:03:10.080
Like I said, I'm specifically

00:03:10.080 --> 00:03:12.990
with the engineering
workforce development team.

00:03:12.990 --> 00:03:17.950
So a lot of what our
goals are include trying

00:03:17.950 --> 00:03:20.750
to develop the future
workforce to be a part

00:03:20.750 --> 00:03:23.250
of the quantum internet
and help develop it

00:03:23.250 --> 00:03:26.690
and also help develop the quantum network.

00:03:26.690 --> 00:03:31.150
So a lot of what we do
is outreach in trying

00:03:31.150 --> 00:03:33.570
to educate people and try
to get people interested

00:03:33.570 --> 00:03:35.490
in the quantum internet and technologies

00:03:35.490 --> 00:03:36.500
and all of this kind of stuff

00:03:36.500 --> 00:03:39.430
that some of you are
gonna hear about today.

00:03:39.430 --> 00:03:43.880
So real quick, if there
are any teachers out there,

00:03:43.880 --> 00:03:46.820
we are looking for some classrooms

00:03:46.820 --> 00:03:48.750
to actually run some virtual field trips

00:03:48.750 --> 00:03:52.490
where students will get to kind
of have an interactive peek

00:03:52.490 --> 00:03:55.460
into some of the coolest
stuff that happens

00:03:55.460 --> 00:03:57.500
in these labs that are
doing things related

00:03:57.500 --> 00:04:00.443
to the quantum internet
and quantum networks.

00:04:01.420 --> 00:04:04.620
And so I don't think
Florian mentioned this,

00:04:04.620 --> 00:04:07.790
but why/how he got interested in quantum,

00:04:07.790 --> 00:04:11.733
but to be honest actually I
have no background in quantum.

00:04:12.620 --> 00:04:15.610
I have a PhD in geology, so
a lot of this is new to me

00:04:15.610 --> 00:04:17.100
and I'm gonna be learning a lot.

00:04:17.100 --> 00:04:21.000
But for me, I think kind
of one of the cool things

00:04:21.000 --> 00:04:23.980
about quantum is just
how interesting it is

00:04:23.980 --> 00:04:27.170
and just kind of the weird crazy stuff

00:04:27.170 --> 00:04:30.320
that goes on with quantum.

00:04:30.320 --> 00:04:31.483
So thanks Florian.

