00:11:04.040 --> 00:11:06.380
So one of the best pieces
of advice I received

00:11:06.380 --> 00:11:08.520
from any professor my first year

00:11:08.520 --> 00:11:10.200
was one of my professors was talking

00:11:10.200 --> 00:11:12.920
about who cow kids who found success

00:11:12.920 --> 00:11:15.700
in their high school career
are often really too scared

00:11:15.700 --> 00:11:18.260
to pivot to something else
and try something new.

00:11:18.260 --> 00:11:21.140
I actually entered Yale
as a neuroscience major.

00:11:21.140 --> 00:11:22.870
I felt very secure in that.

00:11:22.870 --> 00:11:26.070
And then during my first year,
I fell in love with physics

00:11:26.070 --> 00:11:27.790
and I felt like, should I change?

00:11:27.790 --> 00:11:28.623
Should I not?

00:11:28.623 --> 00:11:30.050
And then because of
this professor's advice,

00:11:30.050 --> 00:11:31.400
I decided to go for it.

00:11:31.400 --> 00:11:34.270
And now in my second year,
since I started Yale,

00:11:34.270 --> 00:11:36.810
I'm falling in love with
the intersection of physics

00:11:36.810 --> 00:11:40.130
and computer science,
specifically quantum computing

00:11:40.130 --> 00:11:41.960
and I guess my biggest piece of advice

00:11:41.960 --> 00:11:43.700
is to maintain an open attitude

00:11:43.700 --> 00:11:45.800
to take classes that interest you

00:11:45.800 --> 00:11:47.670
and being willing to embrace changes

00:11:47.670 --> 00:11:48.870
in what you wanna study.

00:11:52.940 --> 00:11:53.993
- Absolutely.

00:11:55.300 --> 00:11:57.460
You know, I think when I entered

00:11:57.460 --> 00:11:58.710
or when I was first interested

00:11:58.710 --> 00:12:00.520
