00:13:08.000 --> 00:13:10.500
that I started up this group.

00:13:10.500 --> 00:13:13.610
The first was to make that community right

00:13:13.610 --> 00:13:16.190
that I talked about
because I was lucky enough

00:13:16.190 --> 00:13:19.100
to have a suite-mate who was
interested in the same thing

00:13:19.100 --> 00:13:23.360
but having like a club, having
sort of an organization there

00:13:24.210 --> 00:13:26.630
for students who are just starting out,

00:13:26.630 --> 00:13:28.130
who are really interested and who need

00:13:28.130 --> 00:13:31.760
that sort of support network
would be very useful.

00:13:31.760 --> 00:13:33.660
So that was the first
thing to build community.

00:13:33.660 --> 00:13:35.190
The second thing was

00:13:35.190 --> 00:13:40.190
because I wanted to reach
people who were interested

00:13:40.280 --> 00:13:42.480
in quantum computing but
they didn't have this sort

00:13:42.480 --> 00:13:44.610
of background in physics
or computer science, right?

00:13:44.610 --> 00:13:46.310
They came from other STEM fields,

00:13:46.310 --> 00:13:48.110
they came from non STEM fields

00:13:48.110 --> 00:13:51.280
and they had that amazing
potential to make impact,

00:13:51.280 --> 00:13:52.113
but they didn't...

00:13:52.113 --> 00:13:53.850
I mean, no one had really
like told them that, you know,

00:13:53.850 --> 00:13:55.720
this is something that you can do.

00:13:55.720 --> 00:13:56.553
So, yeah.

00:13:56.553 --> 00:13:59.120
So it was a cool club where people

00:13:59.120 --> 00:14:02.570
from interdisciplinary
backgrounds could come in

00:14:02.570 --> 00:14:03.403
and create impact.

00:14:03.403 --> 00:14:04.470
And the third reason was

00:14:04.470 --> 00:14:06.037
because I wanted to work on cool projects.

00:14:06.037 --> 00:14:06.870
(Jha chuckles)

00:14:06.870 --> 00:14:08.860
And I wanted to work on
cool projects with people

00:14:08.860 --> 00:14:11.100
from different backgrounds
and different perspectives.

00:14:11.100 --> 00:14:13.310
I think that's where the
coolest projects come from

00:14:13.310 --> 00:14:16.170
when you have people that come
from different backgrounds.

00:14:16.170 --> 00:14:19.860
Yeah. So, and I think all those
three reasons were achieved

00:14:19.860 --> 00:14:22.640
and will continue to, I guess like be met

00:14:22.640 --> 00:14:23.733
in the coming years.

00:14:26.310 --> 00:14:27.143
- Thank you.

00:14:28.360 --> 00:14:30.480
The fact that we're mixing so many people

00:14:30.480 --> 00:14:33.670
across so many disciplines
is also the challenge

00:14:33.670 --> 00:14:36.990
of this technology and this field,

00:14:36.990 --> 00:14:39.260
because we are going to
need a lot of people.

00:14:39.260 --> 00:14:41.330
And so there's not a single...

00:14:41.330 --> 00:14:43.620
It's great for all of you trying

00:14:43.620 --> 00:14:45.230
to consider career in quantum,

00:14:45.230 --> 00:14:47.310
but it's also a little
bit difficult for us

00:14:47.310 --> 00:14:49.960
to give you one answer because
you can enter the field

00:14:49.960 --> 00:14:52.230
in so many different ways.

00:14:52.230 --> 00:14:54.610
That's something that's very challenging.

00:14:54.610 --> 00:14:59.173
And I think the US is trying to help that

00:15:00.740 --> 00:15:05.740
by launching initiative like
the center for quantum network

00:15:05.850 --> 00:15:07.020
for all the different hubs,

00:15:07.020 --> 00:15:09.070
national hubs of the quantum initiatives.

00:15:10.280 --> 00:15:12.330
Is it something that you can
talk a little bit more about,

00:15:12.330 --> 00:15:13.763
Steve, about this center?

00:15:19.270 --> 00:15:20.810
- Yeah, so

00:15:24.922 --> 00:15:27.040
the first thing to understand is

00:15:27.040 --> 00:15:32.040
that quantum computing is at
a very early stage, right?

00:15:33.050 --> 00:15:37.593
You know, maybe we've invented
the quantum Abacus or,

00:15:39.720 --> 00:15:43.700
you know, some very crude
versions of computers exist,

00:15:43.700 --> 00:15:45.950
but they're not that useful yet.

00:15:45.950 --> 00:15:50.120
And so we have many problems to solve

00:15:50.120 --> 00:15:52.580
and it's going to take a while.

00:15:52.580 --> 00:15:57.580
So young people, your age are
needed to come into the field

00:15:59.560 --> 00:16:04.560
to help us solve these problems
over the next 10 or 20 years

00:16:05.890 --> 00:16:07.423
or however long it takes.

00:16:08.720 --> 00:16:13.720
And so the center that I direct, C2 QA

00:16:16.830 --> 00:16:19.620
is a very large national center.

00:16:19.620 --> 00:16:23.873
There are 24 institutions,

00:16:25.438 --> 00:16:27.340
five federal labs,

00:16:27.340 --> 00:16:32.340
18 universities and IBM
as our industrial partner

00:16:32.700 --> 00:16:35.190
because they have experience, you know,

00:16:35.190 --> 00:16:39.360
physicists are not system builders

00:16:40.950 --> 00:16:42.810
or even computer scientists.

00:16:42.810 --> 00:16:45.210
And so, you know, people
with industrial experience

00:16:45.210 --> 00:16:46.933
are of course valuable.

00:16:47.920 --> 00:16:52.920
And what's called the
technology readiness level

00:16:53.320 --> 00:16:54.400
is just very low.

00:16:54.400 --> 00:16:56.640
There are many, many problems to solve

00:16:56.640 --> 00:17:01.640
and you can contribute whether
you're a mechanical engineer

00:17:01.850 --> 00:17:06.293
electrical engineer, a computer
scientist, a physicist,

00:17:08.220 --> 00:17:11.333
a material scientist, a chemist.

00:17:12.880 --> 00:17:17.880
We're trying to invent an
entirely new type of technology

00:17:19.580 --> 00:17:24.580
and no one person or even
no group of people all

00:17:25.040 --> 00:17:28.730
from the same background
could possibly solve

00:17:28.730 --> 00:17:32.970
all the engineering
challenges that we face.

00:17:34.630 --> 00:17:39.630
So it's that, again means
there are many routes

00:17:39.850 --> 00:17:41.640
for you to enter this field.

00:17:41.640 --> 00:17:46.090
And it means that centers
like the one that I direct

00:17:46.090 --> 00:17:50.230
are important precisely because
they bring together people

00:17:50.230 --> 00:17:52.650
who haven't been talking
to each other before.

00:17:52.650 --> 00:17:55.650
So I now have a chance.

00:17:55.650 --> 00:17:58.840
I'm now collaborating
with computer scientists

00:17:58.840 --> 00:18:01.730
that I had, you know, I
just I know very little

00:18:01.730 --> 00:18:04.080
about computer science that they know.

00:18:04.080 --> 00:18:06.640
I know a little, they know
a little bit about physics

00:18:06.640 --> 00:18:10.440
and a little bit about quantum computing

00:18:10.440 --> 00:18:13.390
and we're working together to try to think

00:18:13.390 --> 00:18:17.580
about how do you write
programs for quantum computers?

00:18:17.580 --> 00:18:19.390
How do you compile them?

00:18:19.390 --> 00:18:21.630
How do you get them to work

00:18:21.630 --> 00:18:25.593
even though the hardware
is still not very reliable?

00:18:27.320 --> 00:18:31.233
There are people that
are electronic, you know,

00:18:32.720 --> 00:18:37.133
work with high speed
electronics or people that,

00:18:39.790 --> 00:18:44.690
you know, just come from
all kinds of backgrounds.

00:18:44.690 --> 00:18:49.023
And so it's really an opportunity

00:18:51.510 --> 00:18:56.510
to build communities
with diverse backgrounds

00:18:57.520 --> 00:19:00.433
to try to tackle these big challenges.

00:19:01.900 --> 00:19:04.480
- Well, speaking of that,
like how do you have...

00:19:04.480 --> 00:19:07.950
Like you have all these
avenues to enter this field,

00:19:07.950 --> 00:19:11.560
but like how do you decide
which one is good for you?

00:19:11.560 --> 00:19:13.920
Can one of you panelists tell me,

00:19:13.920 --> 00:19:17.170
like if you had, like, what
kind of experience you had

00:19:17.170 --> 00:19:19.230
to sort of like try different things.

00:19:19.230 --> 00:19:21.750
Like, is there tools
online that you can use?

00:19:21.750 --> 00:19:24.370
Like what kind of activities you can do?

00:19:24.370 --> 00:19:28.730
I'm hinting at maybe a
hackathon, just some means.

00:19:28.730 --> 00:19:30.970
(Florian laughs)

00:19:30.970 --> 00:19:33.167
One of you want to talk about that?

00:19:35.500 --> 00:19:39.500
- Yeah. I can say a bit
about expand on that hint.

00:19:39.500 --> 00:19:41.980
Yeah. We had this fantastic event,

00:19:41.980 --> 00:19:46.170
the quantum coalition hack
that I have helped lead.

00:19:46.170 --> 00:19:49.010
And this is really incredible
event because I mean,

00:19:49.010 --> 00:19:53.240
we had sponsorship from
companies across the world.

00:19:53.240 --> 00:19:57.490
So our biggest sponsors were
IBM and Q-CTRL and Google.

00:19:57.490 --> 00:19:59.490
And we had a ton of other sponsors.

00:19:59.490 --> 00:20:01.320
And I think the coolest thing though,

00:20:01.320 --> 00:20:04.453
was that we had 2000 people sign up,

00:20:06.292 --> 00:20:08.730
1,400 people participate in some way

00:20:08.730 --> 00:20:13.170
and 200 teams of like 3 to
5 people submit projects.

00:20:13.170 --> 00:20:17.440
And this shows how much
interest there is out there.

00:20:17.440 --> 00:20:18.900
And this hackathon

00:20:18.900 --> 00:20:21.680
was conducted completely virtually, right?

00:20:21.680 --> 00:20:24.910
So without needing to go
into a lab, without needing

00:20:24.910 --> 00:20:29.240
to even have a very strong laptop,

00:20:29.240 --> 00:20:32.540
we had sponsors that would
provide online interfaces

00:20:32.540 --> 00:20:34.210
that you could use

00:20:34.210 --> 00:20:37.270
if you didn't have very powerful
computing tools at home.

00:20:37.270 --> 00:20:40.500
I think this is a testament to
how many resources now exist

00:20:40.500 --> 00:20:43.193
on the cloud that are free to use.

00:20:44.270 --> 00:20:49.270
So I also I'm an IBM Qiskit Advocate.

00:20:50.180 --> 00:20:53.880
So I've had some experience
with Qiskit with contributing

00:20:53.880 --> 00:20:57.310
to Qiskit, which is open source.

00:20:57.310 --> 00:21:02.020
And I can (faintly speaking)
at least for Qiskit,

00:21:02.020 --> 00:21:04.556
there's a ton of tutorials.

00:21:04.556 --> 00:21:06.200
Yeah, there's a wonderful online interface

00:21:06.200 --> 00:21:07.693
at IBM quantum experience,

00:21:08.680 --> 00:21:12.540
and there's a really
hybrid community out there.

00:21:12.540 --> 00:21:15.470
So there's like a pupping Slack channel

00:21:15.470 --> 00:21:18.300
where you can just ask, you
know, if you're coming in from,

00:21:18.300 --> 00:21:19.580
if you're an industry professional,

00:21:19.580 --> 00:21:21.240
if you're a college student,

00:21:21.240 --> 00:21:22.660
if you're a high school
student, you can just go there

00:21:22.660 --> 00:21:24.950
and get some help and get started.

00:21:24.950 --> 00:21:28.040
And this is one of the
many tools out there.

00:21:28.040 --> 00:21:32.010
So definitely if you're interested,

00:21:32.010 --> 00:21:35.610
I would say there are tools
out there to support you.

00:21:35.610 --> 00:21:37.850
There are communities
out there to support you.

00:21:37.850 --> 00:21:40.720
And the best way to get started is to go

00:21:40.720 --> 00:21:42.890
through one of these intro tutorials

00:21:42.890 --> 00:21:45.490
if you're interested in
quantum information science.

00:21:47.474 --> 00:21:49.290
- I just wanna add-

00:21:49.290 --> 00:21:50.597
- Go ahead. Go ahead.

00:21:50.597 --> 00:21:51.430
- Thank you so much.

00:21:51.430 --> 00:21:53.360
I just wanna add one
more note on a hackathon.

00:21:53.360 --> 00:21:56.040
So I had just joined UQC as
director of partnerships,

00:21:56.040 --> 00:21:56.960
but as I'd mentioned,

00:21:56.960 --> 00:21:59.440
I'd literally had my first
quantum computing lecture

00:21:59.440 --> 00:22:00.660
a month before.

00:22:00.660 --> 00:22:02.550
And then it was my
responsibility to reach out

00:22:02.550 --> 00:22:05.580
to all of these sponsors and
ask them to join our hackathon.

00:22:05.580 --> 00:22:07.250
And it felt like a pretty big request.

00:22:07.250 --> 00:22:10.010
And I was very nervous being
a beginner to the field,

00:22:10.010 --> 00:22:14.130
but I was amazed by how
dedicated the sponsors were

00:22:14.130 --> 00:22:15.760
to bringing in the next generation

00:22:15.760 --> 00:22:18.470
and specifically to bringing
in new perspectives.

00:22:18.470 --> 00:22:20.110
I think that's kind of what's special

00:22:20.110 --> 00:22:22.330
about the quantum computing community is

00:22:22.330 --> 00:22:24.130
that people really understand the need

00:22:24.130 --> 00:22:26.450
for interdisciplinary collaboration.

00:22:26.450 --> 00:22:28.620
And they're really open
to bringing in beginners.

00:22:28.620 --> 00:22:31.300
That's just been really
reassuring and exciting for me

00:22:31.300 --> 00:22:32.783
as a beginner to this field.

00:22:36.120 --> 00:22:38.520
- So there's all the resources online.

00:22:38.520 --> 00:22:42.640
Like I think if you just
do a quick search on Google

00:22:42.640 --> 00:22:45.713
about these different things
you will be able to find them.

00:22:46.730 --> 00:22:49.711
A few things that we can point out

00:22:49.711 --> 00:22:52.670
is that so IBM has a lot of resource,

00:22:52.670 --> 00:22:56.510
Qiskit has a lot of also
resources and tutorial.

00:22:56.510 --> 00:23:01.423
Google has also a series of videos online.

00:23:02.580 --> 00:23:06.040
Microsoft is starting to
ramp up their programming

00:23:06.040 --> 00:23:07.970
so that they have more and more things.

00:23:07.970 --> 00:23:10.350
And one thing that I can point out it's,

00:23:10.350 --> 00:23:13.390
if you give me a second
so I can change my slides

00:23:13.390 --> 00:23:15.037
there's these school.

00:23:15.037 --> 00:23:19.650
So you can see on my
webcam, the cubitbycubit.org

00:23:19.650 --> 00:23:22.860
is a website that has a ton of resources

00:23:22.860 --> 00:23:25.200
and they are currently hosting summer camp

00:23:25.200 --> 00:23:28.260
for middle school, high
school and college students.

00:23:28.260 --> 00:23:31.190
And we have also a quantum, I mean not we,

00:23:31.190 --> 00:23:33.620
but like they also have
a quantum summer school

00:23:33.620 --> 00:23:35.340
for researchers.

00:23:35.340 --> 00:23:38.230
So there's a lot of things available.

00:23:38.230 --> 00:23:41.220
And one of the advice that I will give you

00:23:41.220 --> 00:23:42.480
if you're interested in the fields

00:23:42.480 --> 00:23:45.170
but you don't know exactly what to do,

00:23:45.170 --> 00:23:47.440
it's really to try to
follow the tutorials.

00:23:47.440 --> 00:23:50.390
There's like some very
short exercise and some,

00:23:50.390 --> 00:23:52.310
like it's almost the shape of a homework

00:23:52.310 --> 00:23:55.700
and you can like starting with a compiler

00:23:55.700 --> 00:23:59.000
and like try to test your very first code

00:23:59.000 --> 00:24:00.670
on the quantum computers.

00:24:00.670 --> 00:24:02.620
And so to follow on that, Steve,

00:24:02.620 --> 00:24:03.653
could you tell us a little bit more,

00:24:03.653 --> 00:24:07.530
because I knew that he
used the IBM experience

00:24:07.530 --> 00:24:09.210
for your classes.

00:24:09.210 --> 00:24:10.910
Can you talk a little bit
more about the interaction

00:24:10.910 --> 00:24:14.774
of the students with
these quantum computers?

00:24:14.774 --> 00:24:19.774
- Sure. So I'm presently
teaching an undergraduate course

00:24:20.650 --> 00:24:23.730
at Yale physics 345

00:24:23.730 --> 00:24:28.200
and you don't have to have
any background in physics

00:24:29.420 --> 00:24:30.253
to take it.

00:24:30.253 --> 00:24:33.470
The only prerequisite
is to know a little bit

00:24:33.470 --> 00:24:35.980
about linear algebra,

00:24:35.980 --> 00:24:39.743
solving sets of simultaneous
equations, basically.

00:24:40.660 --> 00:24:45.660
And so I give some
lectures on the background

00:24:49.200 --> 00:24:51.440
and then when students
have a little background,

00:24:51.440 --> 00:24:55.730
they can start programming and
stewing homework assignments

00:24:55.730 --> 00:24:59.310
on the IBM quantum experience.

00:24:59.310 --> 00:25:01.170
Florian is putting up

00:25:04.900 --> 00:25:07.230
one of the interfaces that you can use.

00:25:07.230 --> 00:25:08.870
And it's very simple,

00:25:08.870 --> 00:25:13.160
drag and drop graphical
interface to start.

00:25:13.160 --> 00:25:14.730
You don't even have to write code.

00:25:14.730 --> 00:25:17.980
You just drop different quantum gates

00:25:17.980 --> 00:25:21.060
or operations into a circuit.

00:25:21.060 --> 00:25:24.127
And there's a lot of online tutorials

00:25:29.280 --> 00:25:34.280
with IBM Q and with Qiskit
to show you how to use this.

00:25:34.310 --> 00:25:39.050
And it's a lot of fun
students really enjoy playing.

00:25:39.050 --> 00:25:40.240
This is completely free.

00:25:40.240 --> 00:25:42.370
You can have access.

00:25:42.370 --> 00:25:47.060
You know, as a professor I'm a member

00:25:47.060 --> 00:25:49.127
of the education network at IBM.

00:25:49.127 --> 00:25:52.280
And so we have a little
extra access to the machines

00:25:52.280 --> 00:25:56.960
but anybody, anywhere in the
world is welcome to log in

00:25:56.960 --> 00:25:59.240
and start playing with these at no charge.

00:25:59.240 --> 00:26:04.180
So if you're curious to
learn a little bit about,

00:26:04.180 --> 00:26:08.040
you know, try your hand at some
simple, very simple programs

00:26:08.040 --> 00:26:11.573
then you can just go
ahead and start today.

00:26:14.560 --> 00:26:16.810
- We have another question
in the Q and A panel

00:26:16.810 --> 00:26:18.203
for you Shantanu.

00:26:19.320 --> 00:26:21.450
So he's exited that the imagings

00:26:21.450 --> 00:26:23.650
that creating an undergrad club

00:26:23.650 --> 00:26:28.230
on such a complex and advance
subject is not pretty easy.

00:26:28.230 --> 00:26:30.950
He would be interested in
knowing how you prepared

00:26:30.950 --> 00:26:33.610
and study, you know, to
like emperor enough people

00:26:33.610 --> 00:26:35.460
to propose the creation of this club.

00:26:37.240 --> 00:26:38.683
- Yeah, it's a good question

00:26:38.683 --> 00:26:40.933
and, you know, something
that I grappled with

00:26:42.312 --> 00:26:44.870
when I was thinking
starting this organization

00:26:44.870 --> 00:26:48.260
You know, because Yale
has a really rich history

00:26:48.260 --> 00:26:51.140
in quantum information science
and quantum engineering.

00:26:51.140 --> 00:26:54.950
And yeah, I thought, you know, is it okay

00:26:54.950 --> 00:26:57.000
for me to create a club like this?

00:26:57.000 --> 00:27:01.810
And I think what motivated me
was we had an interest meeting

00:27:01.810 --> 00:27:04.350
and there were a lot of undergraduates

00:27:05.430 --> 00:27:07.820
that were interested
in such a club, right?

00:27:07.820 --> 00:27:09.300
And they came.

00:27:09.300 --> 00:27:12.680
We actually, as I like to look
at the numbers and we had,

00:27:12.680 --> 00:27:15.140
I think we had like 10
or 15 different majors

00:27:15.140 --> 00:27:18.680
outside of physics and
computer science and math

00:27:19.750 --> 00:27:22.250
that were interested in
joining such a club, right?

00:27:22.250 --> 00:27:25.480
That wouldn't have had access
to these amazing resources,

00:27:25.480 --> 00:27:29.700
this like rich history that
and graduate community at Yale

00:27:29.700 --> 00:27:31.330
if not for a club like this.

00:27:31.330 --> 00:27:34.030
So that's why I continued with it

00:27:34.030 --> 00:27:35.440
and that's how I got the motivation

00:27:35.440 --> 00:27:39.070
and asked for how I got the
resources to support this.

00:27:39.070 --> 00:27:40.500
I find that a lot of the resources

00:27:40.500 --> 00:27:43.910
were already kind of there.

00:27:43.910 --> 00:27:45.790
And I just had to reach out to ask.

00:27:45.790 --> 00:27:48.840
So I asked professor Girvin
and he was really helpful.

00:27:48.840 --> 00:27:51.730
And I asked Florian, he was very helpful.

00:27:51.730 --> 00:27:55.570
And I think the best thing
that I could have done

00:27:55.570 --> 00:27:58.720
and I did was gathered
really talented board

00:27:59.730 --> 00:28:03.320
of people who were like ILA
who were really enthusiastic

