I'm going to pinpoint on that.

00:43:52.210 --> 00:43:55.903
Ayelet you want to
start last word on that?

00:43:57.610 --> 00:43:58.443
- Yeah, sure.

00:43:58.443 --> 00:44:00.337
I do wanna reiterate what I said before

00:44:00.337 --> 00:44:03.710
and what Kelly echoed about
maintaining an open attitude.

00:44:03.710 --> 00:44:05.370
And I also wanna emphasize

00:44:05.370 --> 00:44:07.910
that you can find a way
to contribute to quantum

00:44:07.910 --> 00:44:09.530
if you have a desire to learn.

00:44:09.530 --> 00:44:12.350
It is often an intimidating
field, but if you have

00:44:12.350 --> 00:44:15.070
that desire to learn no
matter how beginner you are,

00:44:15.070 --> 00:44:16.720
you can find a way to contribute.

00:44:20.130 --> 00:44:20.963
- Shantanu?

00:44:22.550 --> 00:44:23.500
- Yeah, absolutely.

00:44:25.160 --> 00:44:30.160
So on, I guess more general
advice note, try to find someone

00:44:31.890 --> 00:44:34.060
with whom you can actually
learn this material.

00:44:34.060 --> 00:44:37.023
That's really helpful to have
someone there to motivate you.

00:44:37.980 --> 00:44:40.000
And on more practical note,

00:44:40.000 --> 00:44:44.100
if you're interested in like
quantum information science

00:44:44.100 --> 00:44:45.860
in particular and actually
running these circuits,

00:44:45.860 --> 00:44:50.770
I would say, the only requisite
would be learning a bit

00:44:50.770 --> 00:44:53.130
about how matrices work with each other.

00:44:53.130 --> 00:44:55.550
So like how they add and
how they multiply vectors.

00:44:55.550 --> 00:44:59.470
And I think that's like
from a practical standpoint.

00:44:59.470 --> 00:45:02.450
And in terms of opportunities,

00:45:02.450 --> 00:45:04.470
you don't have to wait for an internship.

00:45:04.470 --> 00:45:09.057
You don't have to wait for
like some opportunity come up

00:45:10.670 --> 00:45:12.160
in terms of like a formal program.

00:45:12.160 --> 00:45:16.300
You can start contributing
to open source projects.

00:45:16.300 --> 00:45:18.960
For example, if you're
interested in computer science,

00:45:18.960 --> 00:45:21.833
you can find the Qiskit Qithub repos

00:45:25.260 --> 00:45:28.520
and you can start contributing right away.

00:45:28.520 --> 00:45:29.353
So, yeah.

00:45:29.353 --> 00:45:31.450
So don't be afraid to get started.

00:45:31.450 --> 00:45:34.330
Find someone to help motivate you

00:45:34.330 --> 00:45:36.690
and to bounce off ideas with.

00:45:36.690 --> 00:45:40.060
And if you can, if you're interested,

00:45:40.060 --> 00:45:41.800
try to learn a bit about linear algebra,

00:45:41.800 --> 00:45:43.450
I think that'd be really helpful.

00:45:45.190 --> 00:45:46.023
- Russell?

00:45:49.360 --> 00:45:52.160
- So I think I'll echo
kind of some of the stuff

00:45:52.160 --> 00:45:53.160
that's come up here.

00:45:54.416 --> 00:45:58.280
Find a major or find a field
that you're interested in.

00:45:58.280 --> 00:46:01.640
You know, do some research
about the different pathways.

00:46:01.640 --> 00:46:03.190
We've talked about some of them today,

00:46:03.190 --> 00:46:05.150
but I think find something
that you really enjoy

00:46:05.150 --> 00:46:06.660
and that you can be passionate about.

00:46:06.660 --> 00:46:11.660
And you'll find a way in to,
you know, the quantum workforce

00:46:13.110 --> 00:46:14.230
and just keep an eye out

00:46:14.230 --> 00:46:17.300
for different opportunities
to get involved with outreach

00:46:17.300 --> 00:46:19.730
or some of the extracurriculars
that we've talked about.

00:46:19.730 --> 00:46:21.670
I think that's gonna be really important

00:46:21.670 --> 00:46:25.030
because that's more of
informal kind of laid back way

00:46:25.030 --> 00:46:27.020
to kind of test, you know,

00:46:27.020 --> 00:46:29.510
if you're gonna enjoy this
and making connections

00:46:29.510 --> 00:46:31.110
with people too at those events.

00:46:34.130 --> 00:46:34.963
- Kelly?

00:46:36.530 --> 00:46:39.820
- Sure. I guess one maybe
kind of different thing

00:46:39.820 --> 00:46:42.270
that we haven't discussed
that's on my mind this week

00:46:42.270 --> 00:46:45.710
is May 1st as you may have
heard this traditional

00:46:45.710 --> 00:46:47.800
kind of national college decision day.

00:46:47.800 --> 00:46:50.230
So I've been talking to a
lot of high school students

00:46:50.230 --> 00:46:52.930
and parents this week who
are just really worried.

00:46:52.930 --> 00:46:56.660
They don't wanna make the
wrong decision in their words.

00:46:56.660 --> 00:46:58.320
And so I would just say

00:46:59.530 --> 00:47:02.720
don't worry a lot about
something like that.

00:47:02.720 --> 00:47:06.380
There are so many wonderful
programs in the US

00:47:06.380 --> 00:47:09.910
and around the world
related to these fields.

00:47:09.910 --> 00:47:13.920
And really it's just about
finding a place that you feel

00:47:13.920 --> 00:47:15.820
like is a place you wanna
spend the next four years

00:47:15.820 --> 00:47:19.040
that feels comfortable to you,
that feels welcoming to you.

00:47:19.040 --> 00:47:23.620
And so don't worry so much
about picking the wrong school

00:47:23.620 --> 00:47:26.190
or the wrong program or the wrong major.

00:47:26.190 --> 00:47:29.400
You'll find your path as
you go through college.

00:47:29.400 --> 00:47:32.270
And really it's about finding a place

00:47:32.270 --> 00:47:33.220
that feels like home to you.

00:47:33.220 --> 00:47:35.580
So if you're able to do campus visits

00:47:35.580 --> 00:47:37.210
when you're doing your college search

00:47:37.210 --> 00:47:39.230
or talking to other students.

00:47:39.230 --> 00:47:41.170
I know one of the things
I do a lot in my job

00:47:41.170 --> 00:47:44.270
is connect high school
students to current undergrads

00:47:44.270 --> 00:47:45.900
to talk about things like research

00:47:45.900 --> 00:47:48.790
or their experience or
internships or whatever it may be.

00:47:48.790 --> 00:47:51.300
So just have an open mind,

00:47:51.300 --> 00:47:54.220
apply to any place that interests you.

00:47:54.220 --> 00:47:57.740
And then if you can try to visit and focus

00:47:57.740 --> 00:48:01.750
on picking a place that feels like you

00:48:01.750 --> 00:48:03.233
and you'll be successful.

00:48:05.090 --> 00:48:06.340
- Thank you.

00:48:06.340 --> 00:48:08.363
Steve, last word?

00:48:10.890 --> 00:48:15.890
- Yeah. So, you know, just as it's easier

00:48:16.940 --> 00:48:19.360
to learn languages when you're young,

00:48:19.360 --> 00:48:24.360
I think it's easier to learn
mathematics when you're young.

00:48:24.810 --> 00:48:28.810
And so as Shantanu mentioned,

00:48:28.810 --> 00:48:33.660
really the minimum mathematics
you need to know about

00:48:33.660 --> 00:48:36.410
to enter this field is linear algebra,

00:48:36.410 --> 00:48:39.250
how to solve simultaneous equations,

00:48:39.250 --> 00:48:42.033
how to work with matrices and vectors.

00:48:42.900 --> 00:48:47.890
And there are wonderful
websites like Three Blue,

00:48:50.150 --> 00:48:55.150
One Brown, where you can see
amazingly wonderful videos

00:48:56.630 --> 00:48:59.140
that teach you these things.

00:48:59.140 --> 00:49:02.560
And you can start there.

00:49:02.560 --> 00:49:05.913
You can take a course
when you get to college.

00:49:07.300 --> 00:49:12.300
And I would learn that
first, I would, you know,

00:49:13.730 --> 00:49:16.270
probability and statistics turns out

00:49:16.270 --> 00:49:18.700
to also be an important part of quantum

00:49:18.700 --> 00:49:21.630
because there's a certain randomness

00:49:21.630 --> 00:49:24.310
in the way quantum mechanics works.

00:49:24.310 --> 00:49:28.430
And that's a very, very
important area anyway

00:49:28.430 --> 00:49:32.210
in modern data-driven society.

00:49:32.210 --> 00:49:36.910
And so that'll be a skill,
even if you end up not going

00:49:36.910 --> 00:49:39.980
into quantum or not
even going into science,

00:49:39.980 --> 00:49:41.750
it'll make you a better citizen.

00:49:41.750 --> 00:49:46.623
You can understand, you know,

00:49:49.290 --> 00:49:52.400
the statistics that
people are using to argue

00:49:52.400 --> 00:49:53.973
for different policies.

00:49:54.950 --> 00:49:59.950
So I would, if I had to pick
one thing to suggest you study,

00:50:00.860 --> 00:50:03.793
it's discrete mathematics.

